      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wild Horse began in 1869 as a cavalry outpost, later evolving into a railway station and a full town by the mid‑1870s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Horse%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community takes its name from Wild Horse Creek, a nearby stream that once guided early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Horse%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office opened in Wild Horse in 1904 and remains the town’s only federal building today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
